\section{S-expressions}

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} is a value containing an atom or a list. \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m} and \texttt{l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} construct expressions. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} reads one expression; \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s} reads multiple. \texttt{d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} produces a readable representation. Semicolon comments, nested lists, quoted atoms, and \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}n}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}"}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}\textbackslash{}} escapes are supported. Invalid input raises an exception.

\texttt{a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} bridge the existing runtime AST. An AST leaf is represented by \texttt{(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})}; an inner node by \texttt{(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})}. Consequently an S-expression with one atom argument converts to an AST leaf, and bare atoms convert to \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"}. Use \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} itself when that distinction matters. Lists used as e-graph terms require an atom operator head.

\section{E-graphs and equality saturation}

An \texttt{E\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} stores hash-consed e-nodes in union-find e-classes. \texttt{u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} establishes equality; \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d} restores congruence. For example, merging \texttt{a} and \texttt{b} also makes \texttt{(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{})} equivalent. Public IDs remain usable after merges; \texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} returns their current representative.

Rules are S-expressions:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{lisp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
(= (add ?a ?b) (add ?b ?a))
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{?\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e} denotes a pattern variable. A repeated variable must match the same e-class, not necessarily the same textual expression. Quoted \texttt{"\allowbreak{}?\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}"} is data. RHS variables must be bound on the LHS. \texttt{(\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})} is accepted too. Rules match their LHS and introduce their RHS; equality itself is symmetric, but matching in the reverse orientation requires another rule.

C++:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::EGraph graph;
auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
auto rules = dsl::sexpr_rules(R"(
    (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
    (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
)");
auto report = graph.saturate(rules);
assert(report.saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

D:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
