auto report = graph.saturate(rules);
assert(report.saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} returns \texttt{E\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} records (\texttt{r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}s}). The optional third argument to \texttt{s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} observes each successful rule application. It may run more than once for a binding across iterations. \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{})} calls a user rewriter with the graph and representative only when the IDs are equivalent. Thus a user can respond to equality independently of saturation.

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s} bounds iterations, retained nodes, and total applications. The result reports \texttt{S\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{I\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t}, or \texttt{M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t}. A bounded run retains valid partial results. \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t} selects a minimum-node finite expression even when the graph contains cycles. \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} converts that representative back to the runtime AST.

\section{Graphs, terms and reduction}

\texttt{D\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} provides stable node and edge handles, ordered adjacency, mutation, traversal, shortest paths, topological sorting, strongly connected components, and DOT output. It supports cycles, parallel edges, and isolated nodes. Label callbacks adapt arbitrary payloads for DOT rendering.

\texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m} represents first-order function applications and variables, with S-expression parsing, structural matching, simultaneous substitution, and occurs-checked unification. \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m} applies validated rules in insertion order using outermost or innermost traversal. Normalization reports whether it reached normal form or exhausted its step budget.

\texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} represents finite acyclic terms with shared subexpressions. Reduction redirects every incoming edge of a matched node to its replacement, so a shared redex is reduced once for all its occurrences. Callers retain the returned root and explicitly collect unreachable nodes. The general graph supports cycles; term graph reduction requires acyclic terms. Step budgets limit applications, not memory or recursion depth.

See Chapter 37 (Chapter 37) for both language APIs, examples, DOT rendering, root-handle rules, and the distinction between tree rewriting and shared graph reduction.

