\section{Abstract byte I/\allowbreak{}O}

\texttt{I\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{I\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} borrows a backend implementing:

\begin{itemize}
\item{} \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t};
\item{} \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t}.
\end{itemize}

C++ buffers are \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>}; D buffers are \texttt{u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}]} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}]}. Zero read means EOF. Short transfers are valid. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t} and \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l} loop over short transfers; premature EOF, invalid counts, or a non-progressing write raise an exception. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}k} and \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l} are available when the backend supplies them. Backend lifetime is user-managed.

\section{Static regexes and binary streams}

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}"} compiles a Thompson NFA at compile time. Matching treats input as bytes, including NUL and bytes above \texttt{0\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}7\allowbreak{}f}. Supported syntax:

\begin{itemize}
\item{} literals and escaped literals;
\item{} \texttt{.} (any byte), classes/\allowbreak{}ranges, negated classes;
\item{} grouping \texttt{(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})}, alternation \texttt{|}, repetition \texttt{*}, \texttt{+}, \texttt{?};
\item{} beginning/\allowbreak{}end assertions \texttt{\textasciicircum{}}, \texttt{\$};
\item{} \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}H\allowbreak{}H}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}0}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}n}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}t}.
\end{itemize}

There are no capture groups, backreferences, shorthand classes such as \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}d}, or counted repetitions. \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} requires a whole-input match. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x} returns the longest matching end offset from a position; \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} returns the earliest matching span, choosing the longest match at that position.

\texttt{B\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m} owns a byte buffer and cursor. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns a span/\allowbreak{}slice; \texttt{n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} consumes a regex token, leaving the cursor unchanged on failure. Empty tokens raise an exception. \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} appends bytes. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T} and \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} use explicit \texttt{B\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}g} (\texttt{:\allowbreak{}:} in C++). \texttt{l\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} reads until EOF within a size bound; \texttt{s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{})} writes through the abstract I/\allowbreak{}O protocol.

\section{Derive a visitor from an AST}

Handlers are registered with \texttt{o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})}. A handler may take the node, or a compile-time \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g} followed by the node. \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})} derives the accepted tag set from a runtime AST and constructs a template-specialized dispatch table. Missing handlers are rejected immediately; visiting a tag outside the schema is rejected. \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t} visits one node, and \texttt{w\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}k} traverses in \texttt{V\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} or \texttt{P\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r}.

For compile-time schema validation, C++ uses a type-level AST:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto schema = dsl::ast_schema<"add">(dsl::ast_schema<"num">());
auto visitor = dsl::compile_visitor(schema,
    dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
    dsl::on<"num">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

D can use the actual runtime AST type at CTFE:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
enum schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) {}));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

This compiles handler dispatch and validates a static schema; it is not a runtime JIT compiler. Visitor callbacks supply the semantic operations.

\section{Built-in preprocessor and optional Lua}

\texttt{P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r} supports object/\allowbreak{}function macros, balanced arguments, recursive macro suppression, backslash-newline continuation, include directives, and conditional blocks. Macro expansion respects strings, character literals, and C-style comments. Conditional directives are \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}. Conditions support integer constants, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{!}, comparisons, \texttt{\&\allowbreak{}\&}, and \texttt{|\allowbreak{}|}. Other operations are \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}, and \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}.

This is a DSL preprocessor with the documented subset, not a complete C/\allowbreak{}C++ preprocessor: token-pasting, stringification, variadic macros, and arithmetic in \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f} are not implemented. \texttt{i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} is a user callback returning source for a path; \texttt{s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t} returns source for a language and code string. Generated script output is macro-expanded. Expansion/\allowbreak{}include depths are bounded.

For actual Lua embedding, C++ defines \texttt{M\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}A}, supplies the Lua include/\allowbreak{}link flags, and assigns \texttt{p\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})}. D builds with \texttt{-\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a} (LDC), \texttt{-\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a} (DMD), or \texttt{-\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a} (GDC), links Lua 5.3/\allowbreak{}5.4, and assigns \texttt{p\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})}. The user owns/\allowbreak{}configures the Lua state. Each directive returns a string. The bridge restores the Lua stack after success or failure. For example: \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}'\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}'}.

\section{Explicit context-free grammar}

\texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G} has named nonterminals, token terminals, and productions. Its Earley parser handles left recursion, epsilon productions and unordered alternatives. It returns an AST for the first discovered derivation of an ambiguous grammar, or a farthest token position and expected terminals. The user's lexer supplies the input token sequence.

Grammar S-expression notation:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{lisp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
(grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number")))
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Unquoted RHS symbols are nonterminals; quoted symbols are literal tokens. Each RHS list is an alternative; \texttt{(\allowbreak{})} is epsilon. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} loads this notation and validates references. Alternatively, construct \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l} and \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} values, or use \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n} and \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}g} templates. The resulting AST tags are production names; terminal leaves use the tag \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n}.

\section{Enum reflection}

C++ retains the complete embedded \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m} public API described in Chapter

\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{28}
\item{} D provides native reflection through \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t}, checked casts/\allowbreak{}indexing, adjacency, compile-time dispatch/\allowbreak{}fusion, flags and enum-indexed containers. It has no numeric search-range restriction. \texttt{E\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s} and \texttt{E\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} UDAs customize flags, prefixes and display names. D's native formatting and bitwise operators replace the C++ stream/\allowbreak{}operator integrations.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Verification}

From the MuDSL directory:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
cmake -S tests -B build/mudsl-tests -DMuDSL_TEST_LUA=ON
cmake --build build/mudsl-tests
ctest --test-dir build/mudsl-tests --output-on-failure
ldc2 -unittest -main MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -of=mudsl-test
./mudsl-test
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The C++ test suite includes \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} and an optional native Lua bridge test. D's toolkit tests are embedded in its public module; the MuLLIER modules are linked alongside it.
